{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-02-two-numbers-sums-differences-ratios",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Two Numbers: Sums, Differences and Ratios",
    "description": "Use two clues about two unknown numbers to recover their values, ratio, difference, or another requested relation.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with the physical idea of a total plus an extra gap, then introduce ratio parts. Add percentage-based gaps, fractional comparisons, simultaneous equations, quotient-and-remainder language, and the distinction between knowing a ratio and knowing two exact values. The capstone makes the learner reconcile more than one description of the same pair.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach two-number problems from concrete sum-and-gap reasoning before formal equations. Cover half-sum/half-difference, ratio plus total, difference as a percentage of the larger number, fractional comparisons between the two numbers, two simultaneous linear equations, and quotient-with-remainder statements. Make the learner decide which number is larger before using a positive difference. Include a case where only one relation is given and the exact numbers cannot be determined, while a requested ratio can still be known. Check every solution against both original clues."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn how two clues can pin down two numbers.\n\nA total tells how much the pair has together. A gap tells how far apart they are. A ratio tells how the pair is split. We will use the clue that gives the cleanest first move.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two numbers with a known total and gap can be found without a full system.\n\nSuppose their sum is 50 and their difference is 14.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Think of the smaller number twice, plus the extra 14.\n\n50 − 14 = 36\nSmaller = 36 ÷ 2 = 18\nLarger = 18 + 14 = 32\n\nThis is the half-sum/half-difference idea in plain language.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
